FADE IN:

EXT. BRICK'S PATIO - DAY

A gorgeous day. The sun is shining. But BRICK BRADDOCK, retired police detective, is sitting quietly, contemplating -- something.

The SCREEN DOOR slides open and BRICK'S SISTER STEEL, a gangly tomboy, emerges. She's beautiful, but her clothes look like she dug them out of a trash can.

STEEL
(whispering)
Did you get the money?

BRICK
(turning to her)
I thought we'd wait until we--

STEEL
We can't wait. I need it now.

BRICK
Steel, I can't. I--

She grabs his wrist. BRICK looks at her hand on his wrist. She lets go.

STEEL
Brick, I'm in trouble.

INT. BRICK'S KITCHEN - DAY

BRICK pours two cups of coffee and passes one to STEEL. She takes a sip.

STEEL
Thanks.

BRICK
Talk to me.

Steel takes a deep breath.

STEEL
Remember that guy I was seeing? Marcus?

BRICK
The actor?

STEEL
Yeah. Well, he's not really an actor. He's...

She trails off.

BRICK
He's what?

STEEL
He's in the mob, Brick. The actual mob.

BRICK stands up, nearly knocking over his coffee.

BRICK
Jesus, Steel! What were you thinking?

STEEL
I didn't know! He seemed nice!

INT. BRICK'S LIVING ROOM - DAY - CONTINUOUS

BRICK paces back and forth. STEEL sits on the couch.

BRICK
Okay. Okay. We can fix this. How much do you owe them?

STEEL
Fifty thousand.

BRICK stops pacing.

BRICK
Fifty... Steel, that's--

STEEL
I know! I know it's a lot!

BRICK
(sitting down)
I don't have that kind of money.

STEEL
What about your pension?

BRICK
I can't touch that for another five years.

They sit in silence.

STEEL
What am I going to do?

BRICK
(after a beat)
I'll figure something out.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY

BRICK walks down the street, deep in thought. He passes a PAWN SHOP and stops. He looks at his watch -- a Rolex. His father's watch.

INT. PAWN SHOP - DAY

The PAWNBROKER, a grizzled old man, examines the watch through a jeweler's loupe.

PAWNBROKER
Nice piece. I can give you three grand.

BRICK
It's worth ten.

PAWNBROKER
Maybe to a collector. But I gotta turn a profit.

BRICK
Five.

PAWNBROKER
Three-five. Final offer.

BRICK hesitates, then nods.

EXT. MARCUS'S CLUB - NIGHT

A sleek nightclub. A BOUNCER stands at the door. BRICK approaches.

BOUNCER
Can I help you?

BRICK
I'm here to see Marcus.

BOUNCER
He expecting you?

BRICK
No. But tell him it's about Steel.

The BOUNCER speaks into his radio, then nods to BRICK.

BOUNCER
Go ahead.

INT. MARCUS'S OFFICE - NIGHT

MARCUS, 40s, sharp suit, sits behind a desk. BRICK enters.

MARCUS
Detective Braddock. To what do I owe the pleasure?

BRICK
You know why I'm here.

MARCUS
Do I?

BRICK
Steel. She owes you money.

MARCUS
(smiling)
She told you about that, huh?

BRICK
I'm here to pay it off.

MARCUS leans back in his chair.

MARCUS
The whole fifty K?

BRICK
(lying)
Yeah.

MARCUS
Show me.

BRICK hesitates.

BRICK
I need more time.

MARCUS
(standing)
Then we don't have a deal.

BRICK
Give me one week.

MARCUS
(walking around the desk)
One week, Detective. After that, things get... complicated.

He pats BRICK on the shoulder. BRICK leaves.

EXT. CITY STREET - NIGHT

BRICK walks to his car, frustrated. His phone rings. It's STEEL.

BRICK
(into phone)
Yeah?

STEEL (V.O.)
Did you talk to him?

BRICK
I bought us a week.

STEEL (V.O.)
A week? Brick, that's not enough time!

BRICK
I'll figure something out. Trust me.

He hangs up and gets in his car.

INT. BRICK'S CAR - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS

BRICK sits in the driver's seat, thinking. He makes a decision. He dials a number.

BRICK
(into phone)
Hey. It's Brick. I need your help.

FADE OUT.

THE END
